1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,541
Forbidden Hill: The 1984 Dig That Found

2
00:00:03,621 --> 00:00:07,150
14th-Century Singapura on Fort Canning

3
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:11,873
In January 1984 the American
archaeologist John Miksic and a

4
00:00:11,953 --> 00:00:17,077
small team spent ten days digging
test pits on Fort Canning Hill,

5
00:00:17,157 --> 00:00:21,400
beside the shrine known as
the Keramat Iskandar Shah.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,825
For the first few days they
found almost nothing of interest.

7
00:00:25,825 --> 00:00:29,958
Then the colour of the soil
changed, and out came pieces

8
00:00:30,038 --> 00:00:34,100
of blue-and-white Chinese
porcelain made in the 1300s.

9
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:38,267
It was the first hard evidence
that a town had stood on the hill

10
00:00:38,347 --> 00:00:41,651
some five hundred years
before the British arrived,

11
00:00:41,731 --> 00:00:46,575
and that the Singapura of the old
Malay chronicles was more than a story.

12
00:00:46,575 --> 00:00:50,302
In the early 1980s there
were plans to turn Fort

13
00:00:50,382 --> 00:00:54,664
Canning Hill into an
entertainment and cultural centre,

14
00:00:54,744 --> 00:00:58,075
with an underground car park for 300 cars.

15
00:00:58,075 --> 00:01:02,685
Some of the staff at the National
Museum knew the old traditions that

16
00:01:02,765 --> 00:01:07,376
a 14th-century palace had stood on
the hill, and they invited Miksic,

17
00:01:07,456 --> 00:01:11,047
then teaching at Gadjah Mada
University in Yogyakarta,

18
00:01:11,127 --> 00:01:16,225
to see whether anything was left in
the ground before the builders arrived.

19
00:01:16,225 --> 00:01:21,750
The dig ran from 18 to
the 28th of January 1984.

20
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:24,355
Miksic's crew was a mix of museum staff,

21
00:01:24,435 --> 00:01:28,854
hired labourers and national
servicemen who had been put on the job

22
00:01:28,934 --> 00:01:32,210
as a punishment, though
by his account they became

23
00:01:32,290 --> 00:01:36,050
interested once he explained
what they were looking for.

24
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:39,528
Heavy rain slowed the
work, and the first finds

25
00:01:39,608 --> 00:01:43,162
were a goat skeleton, an
old British army helmet

26
00:01:43,242 --> 00:01:46,800
and a few bricks from
the 19th or 20th century.

27
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,544
Then, in Miksic's words,
"suddenly we saw the soil colour

28
00:01:50,624 --> 00:01:54,650
change and we started finding
different kinds of artefacts."

29
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:59,490
Among them were pieces of
blue-and-white porcelain from

30
00:01:59,570 --> 00:02:04,400
the Yuan dynasty, which ruled
China from 1279 to 1368.

31
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,876
In all, the ten days produced
1,346 sherds of pottery,

32
00:02:08,956 --> 00:02:11,914
weighing a little over 14 kilograms,

33
00:02:11,994 --> 00:02:16,550
lying where they had been
dropped in the 14th century.

34
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:20,132
The Straits Times, reporting
on the finds that March,

35
00:02:20,212 --> 00:02:24,831
asked whether the "fabulous kingdom
of Temasek", known from Chinese,

36
00:02:24,911 --> 00:02:28,850
Javanese and Portuguese texts,
had really been Singapore.

37
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:33,063
The plans for the hill were
changed, and the parks authorities

38
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:36,675
later hired Miksic as a
consultant to keep digging.

39
00:02:36,675 --> 00:02:39,970
The finds made sense
of a set of clues that

40
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:43,425
had been piling up for
more than 160 years.

41
00:02:43,425 --> 00:02:47,385
When Stamford Raffles and
William Farquhar landed at the

42
00:02:47,465 --> 00:02:50,775
end of January 1819, they
asked the Temenggong,

43
00:02:50,855 --> 00:02:55,400
the Malay chief of the island,
about the hill behind the plain.

44
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:59,449
Wa Hakim, who was a boy on the
island at the time and told the

45
00:02:59,529 --> 00:03:04,045
story many years later, remembered
the answer: it was Bukit Larangan,

46
00:03:04,125 --> 00:03:08,374
the Forbidden Hill, "because the
Rajah resided there in old times

47
00:03:08,454 --> 00:03:12,650
and erected his palace there,
and would allow no one to go up".

48
00:03:12,650 --> 00:03:15,577
The tombs of the old
rulers were believed to

49
00:03:15,657 --> 00:03:18,800
be on the hill, and it
was held to be haunted.

50
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:23,651
Munshi Abdullah, the Malay scribe who
came to Singapore a few months later,

51
00:03:23,731 --> 00:03:26,543
recorded that when
Farquhar wanted to go up,

52
00:03:26,623 --> 00:03:31,225
none of the Temenggong's people would
go with him, for fear of ghosts.

53
00:03:31,225 --> 00:03:36,006
Farquhar took his Malacca men instead
and had a gun dragged to the top,

54
00:03:36,086 --> 00:03:40,400
where a salute of twelve shots
was fired and a flagpole put up.

55
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:44,975
Then he ordered the hill cleared
and a road cut to the summit.

56
00:03:44,975 --> 00:03:47,326
Raffles later put a bungalow there,

57
00:03:47,406 --> 00:03:51,772
which became Government House,
and for decades afterwards Malays

58
00:03:51,852 --> 00:03:55,325
still climbed the hill to
decorate the old graves.

59
00:03:55,325 --> 00:03:58,495
John Crawfurd, who later became the second

60
00:03:58,575 --> 00:04:02,286
Resident of Singapore,
spent two days in February

61
00:04:02,366 --> 00:04:05,925
1822 looking at what was
left of the old town.

62
00:04:05,925 --> 00:04:10,200
On the morning of the 3rd of
February he walked its boundary.

63
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:13,639
To the north, an earth wall
about 16 feet (5 metres)

64
00:04:13,719 --> 00:04:17,022
wide at the base and still
eight or nine feet high

65
00:04:17,102 --> 00:04:21,150
ran for almost a mile from the
shore to the foot of the hill,

66
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:24,275
with a stream along it
that served as a moat.

67
00:04:24,275 --> 00:04:28,200
The sea and the river creek
made up the other sides.

68
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:33,925
The hill itself, about 150 feet (45 metres)
high, had just been cleared of forest,

69
00:04:34,005 --> 00:04:37,606
and Crawfurd found the west
and north slopes covered

70
00:04:37,686 --> 00:04:42,500
with the foundations of buildings,
some of them of good baked brick.

71
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:46,045
The most striking was a
square terrace about 40

72
00:04:46,125 --> 00:04:49,750
feet (12 metres) on each
side, near the summit.

73
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:53,345
Around its edge lay fourteen
large blocks of sandstone,

74
00:04:53,425 --> 00:04:58,022
each with a hole in it, which he
took to be the bases of wooden posts,

75
00:04:58,102 --> 00:05:01,175
so the building above
had been made of timber.

76
00:05:01,175 --> 00:05:04,227
In the middle was a ring
of rough stones around a

77
00:05:04,307 --> 00:05:06,912
hollow that might once have held an image,

78
00:05:06,992 --> 00:05:10,700
and he guessed the whole thing
had been a Buddhist temple.

79
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:14,405
A second terrace on the north
slope was said to be the

80
00:05:14,485 --> 00:05:17,980
grave of Iskandar Shah,
the last king of Singapura,

81
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,695
and a simple shelter had
already been put up over it,

82
00:05:21,775 --> 00:05:25,350
where Muslims, Hindus and
Chinese all came to pray.

83
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:28,944
On the east side of the
hill Crawfurd found durian,

84
00:05:29,024 --> 00:05:32,042
rambutan, duku and
pomelo trees, gone wild,

85
00:05:32,122 --> 00:05:36,300
which he believed had been
planted by the old inhabitants.

86
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:40,453
Among the ruins lay broken
Chinese and local pottery,

87
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:44,925
and Chinese brass coins of
the 10th and 11th centuries.

88
00:05:44,925 --> 00:05:48,303
The Sejarah Melayu, the
Malay Annals, tells how a

89
00:05:48,383 --> 00:05:52,326
prince from Palembang named
Sang Nila Utama landed on the

90
00:05:52,406 --> 00:05:56,419
island of Temasek, saw a strange
beast he took for a lion,

91
00:05:56,499 --> 00:05:59,675
and named the place
Singapura, the Lion City.

92
00:05:59,675 --> 00:06:04,121
Five kings ruled there, the Annals
say, until the last of them,

93
00:06:04,201 --> 00:06:07,066
Iskandar Shah, lost the city to an attack

94
00:06:07,146 --> 00:06:11,025
from Java after one of his
own ministers betrayed him.

95
00:06:11,025 --> 00:06:15,875
The king fled up the coast, and
his line went on to found Melaka.

96
00:06:15,875 --> 00:06:22,143
The Annals date none of this, and the
dates often given for the five reigns,

97
00:06:22,223 --> 00:06:26,650
from 1299 to 1398, are a
20th-century reconstruction.

98
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:29,475
There is one outside witness.

99
00:06:29,475 --> 00:06:32,982
Around 1330 the Chinese
traveller Wang Dayuan

100
00:06:33,062 --> 00:06:35,692
visited a place he called Danmaxi,

101
00:06:35,772 --> 00:06:40,475
usually taken to be Temasek,
and described two settlements.

102
00:06:40,475 --> 00:06:45,100
One, Longyamen, lay by the
strait and was home to pirates.

103
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:49,648
The other, Banzu, lay on a hill
behind it, and its people were honest,

104
00:06:49,728 --> 00:06:53,100
wore their hair short and
had a chief of their own.

105
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:56,519
Banzu may be a Chinese
rendering of the Malay pancur,

106
00:06:56,599 --> 00:06:59,886
a spring, and Fort Canning
had one: a spring on the

107
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:03,385
west side of the hill, known
as the forbidden spring,

108
00:07:03,465 --> 00:07:06,700
where the ruler's women
were said to have bathed.

109
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:11,068
Wang also wrote that a Siamese
fleet of about 70 ships had

110
00:07:11,148 --> 00:07:15,750
besieged the place for a month
a few years before his visit.

111
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:19,920
For most of the 20th century,
many historians treated the

112
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,250
Annals' account of Singapura
as more legend than history.

113
00:07:24,250 --> 00:07:28,370
They were written down two centuries
after the events they describe,

114
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:29,853
for the court of Melaka,

115
00:07:29,933 --> 00:07:33,312
and apart from Crawfurd's
ruins and the Singapore Stone,

116
00:07:33,392 --> 00:07:36,975
there was very little on the
ground to check them against.

117
00:07:36,975 --> 00:07:41,803
On the 7th of July 1928, labourers
clearing the summit of Fort

118
00:07:41,883 --> 00:07:46,950
Canning for a reservoir dug up a
cache of eleven gold ornaments.

119
00:07:46,950 --> 00:07:50,913
There was a pair of armlets
with flexible chains, a clasp,

120
00:07:50,993 --> 00:07:55,525
an elliptical ornament, an inscribed
ring and several more rings.

121
00:07:55,525 --> 00:07:59,568
The engineer on the site
recorded that they lay just below

122
00:07:59,648 --> 00:08:03,700
the top of the soil laid down
before the British arrived.

123
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:07,934
The finest piece was an armlet
with a plaque showing a kala,

124
00:08:08,014 --> 00:08:12,975
the guardian face that Javanese
builders carved over temple doorways.

125
00:08:12,975 --> 00:08:16,440
The Dutch archaeologist P.
V. van Stein Callenfels,

126
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:20,610
asked for his opinion, identified
the work as East Javanese,

127
00:08:20,690 --> 00:08:25,058
from the time of the Majapahit
empire, and the historian Richard

128
00:08:25,138 --> 00:08:29,100
Winstedt described the find
in a short article that year.

129
00:08:29,100 --> 00:08:32,493
Only the armlet and a pair
of earrings are in the

130
00:08:32,573 --> 00:08:35,975
National Museum today;
the rest were later lost.

131
00:08:35,975 --> 00:08:39,243
A study published in 2023 by Natalie Ong

132
00:08:39,323 --> 00:08:43,425
questioned whether the
armlet is Javanese at all.

133
00:08:43,425 --> 00:08:47,496
The gold showed that someone
wealthy had lived on the hill in

134
00:08:47,576 --> 00:08:51,987
the 14th century, but it was a
single hoard with no record of what

135
00:08:52,067 --> 00:08:56,150
lay around it, and on its own
it proved little about a town.

136
00:08:56,150 --> 00:09:00,339
The 1984 test pits were
followed by seven more

137
00:09:00,419 --> 00:09:04,887
excavations on the hill
by 1995, including one in

138
00:09:04,967 --> 00:09:09,700
1987 run as part of an
ASEAN archaeology programme.

139
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:13,947
Miksic joined the National
University of Singapore that year,

140
00:09:14,027 --> 00:09:18,425
and from then on members of the
public could volunteer to dig.

141
00:09:18,425 --> 00:09:22,736
Over the years the hill produced
more than 10,000 glass beads,

142
00:09:22,816 --> 00:09:26,772
fine white Chinese porcelain
with cobalt-blue decoration,

143
00:09:26,852 --> 00:09:31,871
fragments of elaborate glass vessels
from China, and a Chinese porcelain

144
00:09:31,951 --> 00:09:36,200
compass that is described as
the only one of its kind known.

145
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:40,158
The diggers also found a
workshop where gold was worked

146
00:09:40,238 --> 00:09:44,275
and broken glass was melted
down and made into bangles.

147
00:09:44,275 --> 00:09:48,359
Taken together, the finds
point to a high-status quarter,

148
00:09:48,439 --> 00:09:52,303
used through the 14th century
and into the early 15th,

149
00:09:52,383 --> 00:09:57,350
which fits with the Annals' account
of the city falling around 1400.

150
00:09:57,350 --> 00:10:01,565
A ten-week dig led by Goh
Geok Yian and Miksic in 2018

151
00:10:01,645 --> 00:10:06,100
found more of the same kinds
of stoneware and porcelain.

152
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:08,633
Later digs showed that the old town

153
00:10:08,713 --> 00:10:11,475
did not stop at the foot of the hill.

154
00:10:11,475 --> 00:10:15,380
Between November 1995 and
January 1996, before the new

155
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:19,218
Parliament House was built,
an excavation beside the

156
00:10:19,298 --> 00:10:23,351
river found layers of sand
and charcoal from the 14th to

157
00:10:23,431 --> 00:10:28,001
the 16th centuries, with debris
from copper and bronze working,

158
00:10:28,081 --> 00:10:31,550
fish hooks, and Chinese
coins in large numbers.

159
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:37,353
In 1998, before the Empress Place Building
became the Asian Civilisations Museum,

160
00:10:37,433 --> 00:10:41,274
a dig on the riverbank there
found goods from Vietnam,

161
00:10:41,354 --> 00:10:45,486
Thailand, Java, Borneo, the
Philippines, China and Persia,

162
00:10:45,566 --> 00:10:50,650
the remains of a place where cargo
was loaded, unloaded and passed on.

163
00:10:50,650 --> 00:10:54,042
Further digs in 2003, at
a corner of the Singapore

164
00:10:54,122 --> 00:10:58,763
Cricket Club's ground on the Padang
and at Saint Andrew's Cathedral,

165
00:10:58,843 --> 00:11:00,915
found older material there too,

166
00:11:00,995 --> 00:11:05,300
a sign that the settlement spread
well beyond the river mouth.

167
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:09,825
Miksic went on digging in Singapore
for the rest of his career.

168
00:11:09,825 --> 00:11:14,111
His book Singapore and
the Silk Road of the Sea,

169
00:11:14,191 --> 00:11:20,113
1300 to 1800 (2013) won the first
Singapore History Prize in 2018,

170
00:11:20,193 --> 00:11:24,650
and he received the Public
Service Medal in 2023.

171
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:28,781
He died on the 25th of October 2025,

172
00:11:28,861 --> 00:11:30,850
at the age of 79.

173
00:11:30,850 --> 00:11:33,082
"We were not just a minor fishing

174
00:11:33,162 --> 00:11:35,825
village," he once told an interviewer.

175
00:11:35,825 --> 00:11:38,950
"We were actually an important place."

176
00:11:38,950 --> 00:11:42,386
The Keramat Iskandar Shah
still stands on the hill,

177
00:11:42,466 --> 00:11:46,523
a tiled wooden pavilion over a
cenotaph, and scholars do not

178
00:11:46,603 --> 00:11:50,590
think it is a real grave:
Iskandar Shah is believed to have

179
00:11:50,670 --> 00:11:54,600
died in Melaka, and no burial
has been found at the site.

180
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:58,308
Part of the excavation near
the summit has been kept open

181
00:11:58,388 --> 00:12:02,096
under a roof, so visitors can
look down into the trenches

182
00:12:02,176 --> 00:12:05,685
and see the layers of soil
the diggers worked through,

183
00:12:05,765 --> 00:12:09,340
and the Sang Nila Utama Garden
nearby has been laid out

184
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:13,275
with a split gateway in the
style of old Javanese temples.

185
00:12:13,275 --> 00:12:16,282
Much of the rest of the
hill's later history,

186
00:12:16,362 --> 00:12:20,191
from Raffles's house to the
forts and the wartime bunker,

187
00:12:20,271 --> 00:12:23,975
sits on top of the ground
that Miksic's team dug into.

188
00:12:23,975 --> 00:12:28,054
Why it matters today: For a
long time Singapore's story

189
00:12:28,134 --> 00:12:32,288
was usually told as starting
in 1819, when Raffles found

190
00:12:32,368 --> 00:12:36,300
what was often described
as a small fishing village.

191
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:40,440
The 1984 dig, and the ones that
followed, showed that there had

192
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:45,129
been a working port town here in the
14th century, trading with China,

193
00:12:45,209 --> 00:12:49,283
Java, India and beyond, with a
high-status quarter on the hill

194
00:12:49,363 --> 00:12:53,650
that its later inhabitants still
remembered as forbidden ground.

195
00:12:53,650 --> 00:12:57,228
When Singapore marked
200 years since Raffles's

196
00:12:57,308 --> 00:13:02,521
landing in 2019, the commemoration
was framed as a 700-year history,

197
00:13:02,601 --> 00:13:06,335
and its centrepiece, the
Bicentennial Experience,

198
00:13:06,415 --> 00:13:09,450
was staged on Fort Canning Hill itself.

199
00:13:09,450 --> 00:13:12,768
Most of that older story
rests on a few square

200
00:13:12,848 --> 00:13:16,025
metres of soil, dug
out a trowel at a time.
